      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that their least common multiple (LCM) is 36 and their greatest common divisor (GCD) is 6. We can use the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two numbers, which is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the relationship above, we can write:\n\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n216 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that their product is 216 and their GCD is 6. Since the GCD is 6, both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be multiples of 6. Let's express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in terms of their GCD:\n\n\\[\na = 6m \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 6n\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime (i.e., \\(\\text{GCD}(m, n) = 1\\)). Substituting these into the product equation:\n\n\\[\n(6m) \\times (6n) = 216\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\n36mn = 216\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(mn\\):\n\n\\[\nmn = \\frac{216}{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nWe need to find two coprime pairs \\((m, n)\\) such that \\(mn = 6\\). The pairs of factors of 6 are:\n\n\\[\n(1, 6), (2, 3), (3, 2), (6, 1)\n\\]\n\nWe need to check which of these pairs are coprime. Clearly, 1 and 6 are not coprime, 2 and 3 are coprime:\n\n\\[\nm = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are:\n\n\\[\na = 6m = 6 \\times 2 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 6n = 6 \\times 3 = 18\n\\]\n\nFinally, we verify that the LCM of 12 and 18 is 36 and their GCD is 6:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(12, 18) = \\frac{12 \\times 18}{\\text{GCD}(12, 18)} = \\frac{216}{6} = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(12, 18) = 6\n\\]\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied. Therefore, the product of the two numbers is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
